# gateway (Traefik) — no secrets.
#
# ACME credentials for a DNS-01 challenge go in a per-provider env file that is
# NOT committed and NOT this one; the gateway is the only container that should
# ever hold them, which is the entire argument for per-service env files.
TZ=UTC

# The host the public API answers on. Every router in
# gateway/dynamic/public-api.yml matches `Host({{ env "CF_API_HOST" }})`.
#
# IT HAS TO BE DEFINED HERE, in the env_file the gateway actually loads.
# It was not, and the failure is silent rather than loud: with the variable
# unset the template renders an EMPTY host and every public router becomes
# `Host(``) && PathPrefix(...)`, which is a valid rule that matches no request
# ever sent. Traefik logs no error. The whole public API is simply dead, and
# the only symptom is a 404 from the catch-all.
#
# It went unnoticed because the verification recipe in README.md passes
# `-e CF_API_HOST=api.example.com` on the command line — so the check supplied
# the value the deployment does not, and proved something the deployment could
# not do.
#
# ── IT MUST BE A SUBDOMAIN OF CF_WEB_APEX, AND IT WAS NOT ─────────────────────
#
# This used to be `api.cloudsforge.online` — "the production host from the CORS
# allowlist; a local stack wants api.localhost" — while `CF_WEB_APEX` below is
# `cloudsforge.localtest.me`. So the whole public API was routed on a hostname
# NOTHING IN THIS ESTATE RESOLVES, and the address the estate's own registry
# declares for it answered 404:
#
#     https://api.cloudsforge.localtest.me/v1/rates   -> 404, before this change
#
# `api` is a REGISTERED SURFACE — `ui/packages/ui/src/surfaces.ts`, subdomain
# `api`, "The public v1 surface" — so `cloudsforgeHosts().api` on any page this
# gateway serves is `https://api.<apex>`, and that is the door the SDK, the
# developer portal and every third-party example compose. Pinning the router to a
# different apex is the same class of defect as the empty `Host(``)` this file's
# comment above describes, one step less invisible: it fails on the right host
# instead of on none.
#
# The two are kept in step by `scripts/estate-up.sh`, which derives this from
# `CF_WEB_APEX` and refuses to start when the two files disagree — the same
# preflight that already guards the apex itself. Overriding it for a real
# deployment is still one line, and a deployment whose API host is genuinely not
# under its web apex sets it here and the preflight says so out loud.
CF_API_HOST=api.cloudsforge.online

# The apex the FIFTEEN BROWSER SURFACES are served on.
#
# Read by gateway/dynamic/estate-web.yml (every surface router) and by
# gateway/dynamic/policy.yml (the CORS allowlist block for this apex). It is the
# same variable docker-compose.estate.yml interpolates into
# IDENTITY_HANDOFF_ORIGINS, and the two must agree or a browser signs in at Hub
# and cannot hand off to any other surface.
#
# `cloudsforge.localtest.me` is a PUBLIC wildcard resolving to 127.0.0.1, so a
# dev estate needs no /etc/hosts entry and no sudo: hub.cloudsforge.localtest.me,
# nimbus.cloudsforge.localtest.me and the rest all reach loopback. That is what
# makes the production shape — `cloudsforgeHosts()` deriving every sibling host
# by stripping a known subdomain — testable on a laptop at all.
#
# UNSET IS A SUPPORTED MODE AND IT MEANS "SERVE NO SURFACES". estate-web.yml
# wraps every router in `{{ if env "CF_WEB_APEX" }}` precisely so that an unset
# value produces NO routers rather than fifteen whose rule reads Host(``) — the
# failure this file's CF_API_HOST comment above exists to describe, which Traefik
# does not log and which looks exactly like a 404 from a catch-all.
CF_WEB_APEX=cloudsforge.online
CF_WEB_SUFFIX=.cloudsforge.online
CF_SITE_HOST=cloudsforge.online

# ── HOW A SURFACE'S HOSTNAME IS FORMED, WHICH IS THREE VARIABLES ─────────────
#
# Changed 2026-08-05. An environment used to be a PREFIX ON THE APEX and is now a
# SUFFIX ON THE SUBDOMAIN, because the prefix form was configured and
# unreachable: Cloudflare's Universal SSL is `*.cloudsforge.online` plus the
# apex, a wildcard matches exactly ONE label, and so `hub.testnet.cloudsforge.online`
# failed the TLS handshake at Cloudflare's edge before a request ever reached
# this box. Advanced Certificate Manager covers two labels, is paid, and is not
# bought. `hub-testnet.cloudsforge.online` is one label and is already covered.
#
#   CF_WEB_APEX    the DNS zone. THE SAME IN BOTH ENVIRONMENTS now. Read by
#                  `scripts/gateway-cert.sh` (the `*.<apex>` SAN) and by the
#                  preflights in `scripts/estate-up.sh`. NOT read by
#                  `gateway/dynamic/` any more — the two below are.
#
#   CF_WEB_SUFFIX  everything after a surface's own name. `hub` + this is the
#                  hostname Hub is served on. Every router in
#                  `gateway/dynamic/estate-web.yml` and every origin in
#                  `gateway/dynamic/policy.yml` is `<subdomain>{{ CF_WEB_SUFFIX }}`.
#
#   CF_SITE_HOST   the marketing site's hostname. It needs its own variable
#                  because its registry subdomain is the EMPTY STRING, and
#                  "empty + suffix" would be `-testnet.cloudsforge.online`, which
#                  is not a legal DNS label. The environment label stands alone
#                  instead: `testnet.cloudsforge.online`.
#
# THE THREE OVERLAP AND ARE THEREFORE CHECKED. `scripts/estate-up.sh` refuses to
# start unless CF_WEB_SUFFIX ends in `.$CF_WEB_APEX`, its head is either empty or
# `-<label>`, and CF_SITE_HOST is the same environment's apex surface. Two
# variables holding the same zone is a drift risk; a five-line preflight is
# cheaper than the alternative, which is a template action inside a YAML comment
# (see the header of estate-web.yml for what that costs).
#
# WHY NOT ONE VARIABLE AND A CONDITIONAL. Because the conditional would live in
# `gateway/dynamic/`, where the file provider renders Go templates before parsing
# YAML and where an unbalanced action takes EVERY file in the directory down at
# once. These three are plain strings, non-empty in every environment, which is
# also what `scripts/surface-routes.py` check 6 requires — it rejects an empty
# value as indistinguishable from unset, and a suffix variable whose mainnet
# value was the empty string would have forced that check to be weakened.


# ── THIS GATEWAY TERMINATES ON THE ESTATE LEAF, AND SO DOES TESTNET ──────────
#
# `CF_TLS_DEFAULT_CERT=origin` WAS HERE and is gone, with the templating in
# `gateway/dynamic/tls.yml` that read it. It named a Cloudflare Origin CA leaf,
# installed to stop the tunnel answering 502 — which it did not do, because
# cloudflared validates against the hostname in its service URL and that URL is
# the IP literal `127.0.0.1`. No Origin CA signs an IP literal.
#
# The 502 is fixed instead by the `tunnel` entrypoint
# (compose/docker-compose.gateway.yml): cloudflared is given an `http://` service
# URL, so that hop does no handshake and needs no certificate. With it plaintext
# there is one issuer in this estate again, both environments serve
# `estate.crt`, and this file and its testnet twin no longer disagree about
# certificates at all.
#
# 443 still terminates — see the tail of `gateway/dynamic/tls.yml` for the six
# internal consumers that depend on it and do not go through the tunnel.


# ── THE CHAIN'S TWO UPSTREAMS ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Read by `gateway/dynamic/estate-web.yml`, which routes `rpc.<apex>` and
# `p2p.<apex>` to them. Both are wrapped in `{{ if env … }}` there, so an unset
# value produces NO ROUTER rather than one pointed at an empty URL — the same
# shape, and for the same reason, as CF_WEB_APEX above. `scripts/surface-routes.py`
# check 6 fails when a variable that directory reads is missing from this file or
# from its testnet twin, so an environment cannot lose one of these silently.
#
# WHY THEY ARE `host.docker.internal` AND NOT A CONTAINER NAME. The chain is
# `hearth`, the frozen upstream repository, run from its own compose file by
# `scripts/ember-testnet.sh` on its own `hearth-testnet` network. The estate has
# always reached it across the host — `docker-compose.estate.yml:971` gives the
# indexer `${EMBER_RPC_URL:-http://host.docker.internal:8545}` and says why —
# and these two are that same fact for the gateway. THEY MUST AGREE WITH
# `EMBER_RPC_URL`: the indexer and the public endpoint disagreeing about which
# node is the chain is a reorg nobody can see.
#
# 8545 is Hearth's Ethereum JSON-RPC (`hearth/node/src/params.js:327`), NOT 8645,
# which is the REST API and the legacy `{method:'getinfo'}` RPC on the same node.
#
# ── THIS PARAGRAPH SAID TWO THINGS THAT ARE NO LONGER TRUE ───────────────────
#
# It read: "There is exactly one EMBER network in existence — chain id 7412 —
# and this file is the DEV estate's: `CF_WEB_APEX` above is
# `cloudsforge.localtest.me`, not `cloudsforge.online`."
#
# Both halves have expired. `CF_WEB_APEX` above is `cloudsforge.online`: this is
# the file the LIVE mainnet gateway loads, and it argued from a value it had
# stopped holding. And there are TWO chains now — `compose/testnet.env` records
# the split itself ("Mainnet now runs `hearth` / 7411; this environment keeps
# `hearth-testnet` / 7412"), brought up by `docker-compose.hearth-seed.yml` and
# `docker-compose.hearth-seed-testnet.yml` respectively.
#
# So 8545 is correct here for the reason the old text could not give: it is the
# MAINNET seed's Ethereum JSON-RPC, in the mainnet gateway's env file, and it is
# the node the mainnet indexer in this same project already follows. Its testnet
# twin is 8745, in `traefik.testnet.env`, and that file says so.
#
# THE DAY A REAL MAINNET CHAIN EXISTS, THIS LINE IS THE ONE THAT MUST CHANGE, and
# nothing mechanical will notice if it does not: the two nodes speak an identical
# protocol, so a mainnet apex serving a testnet chain answers every request
# correctly and every one of them about the wrong chain. `CF_WEB_APEX` and this
# variable have to be moved together.
#
# ── AND `host.docker.internal` IS NOW A DEFAULT RATHER THAN A FACT ────────────
#
# It stopped being true on 2026-08-10, for this deployment: micro-org#338 moved
# the application stack to a second machine and left the chains where they are, so
# the seed is not on this host at all any more and no host-gateway alias can reach
# it. `CF_CHAIN_HOST` carries the address that can — a WireGuard address, because
# this node has no authentication of any kind and a LAN address would publish
# `eth_call` to the switch.
#
# The three upstreams below all read it, so the machine is named ONCE. That
# matters more than it looks: this file's own paragraph above requires them to
# agree with `EMBER_RPC_URL`, and the way they stop agreeing is somebody moving
# two of three. An unset `CF_CHAIN_HOST` keeps the single-machine behaviour
# exactly, so the estate still comes up unchanged on a host that runs its own node.
CF_RPC_UPSTREAM=http://${CF_CHAIN_HOST:-host.docker.internal}:8545

# 8648 is the WebSocket P2P transport, under `HEARTH_P2P_WS`, path `/p2p`. It is a
# contract with hearth and not a free choice: 8646 is the raw-TCP peer port a
# tunnel cannot carry, and 8647/8649 are already the two miners' REST ports on
# this host (`hearth/docker-compose.testnet.yml`).
CF_P2P_WS_UPSTREAM=http://${CF_CHAIN_HOST:-host.docker.internal}:8648

# ── THE MINING WORK API, WHICH IS A DIFFERENT PORT FROM THE ONE ABOVE ─────────
#
# `CF_RPC_UPSTREAM` is the ETHEREUM JSON-RPC port (`hearth/node/src/params.js:366`,
# `DEFAULT_JSONRPC_PORT: 8545`). The pool-style mining work API is NOT on it — it
# is on the REST port, `params.js:306`, `DEFAULT_RPC_PORT: 8645`, described there
# as "HTTP REST + legacy JSON-RPC + SSE". Two ports, one node.
#
# So `rpc.<apex>` published the chain and did not publish the two endpoints a
# remote miner actually calls, and no amount of pointing at 8545 would have
# reached them: `GET /mining/template` (`hearth/node/src/evmnode.js:403`) and
# `POST /mining/submit` (`:418`) are only ever served on this one.
#
# `CF_CHAIN_HOST` for the same reason the line above uses it: the node is not in
# this compose project, and since micro-org#338 it is not on this machine either.
# `hearth-testnet-seed` publishes 8645, now on the WireGuard address as well.
CF_MINING_UPSTREAM=http://${CF_CHAIN_HOST:-host.docker.internal}:8645

# ── WHICH EMBER CHAIN THIS ESTATE IS, AND THEREFORE WHETHER IT HAS A FAUCET ───
#
# Read by `gateway/dynamic/estate-web.yml`, which routes `network.<apex>/v1` to
# the faucet ONLY when this says `testnet`. The whole argument is written beside
# that router; the short version is that `faucet/src/env.ts:63` fixes the
# service's network to `testnet` with an `as const` so `NETWORK === 'mainnet'` is
# a compile error, both faucet services carry `profiles: ["ember-testnet"]`, and
# the mainnet estate therefore runs none. Routing to it anyway answered 502 —
# the estate advertising a faucet that cannot exist.
#
# IT MUST AGREE WITH THE SAME VARIABLE IN THE COMPOSE ENVIRONMENT.
# `docker-compose.estate.yml` already keys `env/chain.${CF_EMBER_NETWORK}.env`,
# the indexer's node and `LEDGER_RECONCILE_NETWORK` on it, and its default there
# is `mainnet` — which is what this project is. `compose/testnet.env:126` sets
# `testnet` for the other one, and its `traefik.testnet.env` says the same.
# Disagreement here does not fail: it publishes a faucet route in an estate with
# no faucet, or withholds one from an estate that has it, and
# `estate-verify.sh`'s faucet section is what fails on either.
#
# It is SET rather than left unset for mainnet on purpose. `scripts/surface-routes.py`
# check 6 refuses an empty value in exactly these words: "An environment that
# genuinely should not carry that router changes the ROUTER, where the reason can
# be written down — not this file, where its absence looks like an oversight."
CF_EMBER_NETWORK=mainnet

# ── IS THIS ESTATE RETIRED? MAINNET'S ANSWER IS NO, AND IT IS WRITTEN DOWN ────
#
# Read by `gateway/dynamic/estate-web.yml`, which renders the retirement routers
# — every `*-testnet.cloudsforge.online` hostname 302'd to its mainnet sibling —
# ONLY when this says `true`. Mainnet is the estate being redirected TO, so it
# must never carry them, and `false` is how it says so.
#
# IT IS SET RATHER THAN OMITTED, and that is the whole point of the line. The
# router used to be a bare `{{ if env "CF_WEB_RETIRED" }}`, so mainnet's only way
# to drop it was to leave this variable out — and `scripts/surface-routes.py`
# check 6 refuses exactly that, in exactly these words: an unset variable and an
# empty one are indistinguishable to Traefik's file provider, which is how the
# whole public API once rendered as `Host(``)` and answered nothing while logging
# nothing. deploy CI was red on this file for that reason. The router now
# compares a value, so both environments answer the question and neither can be
# silent about which one it is.
CF_WEB_RETIRED=false

# ── THE TWO CHAIN READ SURFACES: an address index and a contract verifier ────
#
# `compose/docker-compose.hearth-devkit.yml` runs both, on THIS host, on the
# estate's own network — so unlike the three chain upstreams above these are
# compose service names resolved by Docker's embedded DNS, not `CF_CHAIN_HOST`
# over WireGuard. The services themselves reach the chain through
# `EMBER_RPC_URL`, which is where this environment already states which chain it
# is; nothing here needs to repeat that and nothing here should.
#
# ⚠ IDENTICAL STRINGS TO `traefik.testnet.env`, AND THAT IS NOT THE COPY-PASTE
# DEFECT THE THREE CHAIN UPSTREAMS ABOVE GUARD AGAINST. Those must differ because
# one hostname resolves to one node and a wrong port publishes the other chain.
# These resolve on a NETWORK — `${CF_PROJECT}_default` — and the two estates are
# two compose projects with two networks, each running its own pair of
# containers. The isolation is the network, not the string. `CF_PROJECT` set
# wrong fails by resolving nothing, not by quietly serving the other estate's
# index.
#
# MAINNET RUNS THE PAIR TOO, and the pool that motivated them is on testnet. That
# is deliberate: the index answers "every transaction of this address", which
# 7411 cannot answer either and which the estate's own explorer surface wants;
# and the verifier makes every Forge Create token on mainnet readable, since it
# matches on runtime bytecode and every one of those tokens is the same contract.
CF_EXPLORER_INDEX_UPSTREAM=http://hearth-explorer-api:9647
CF_VERIFY_UPSTREAM=http://hearth-verify:9648
